  "prompt_text": "The figure compares two solar-cell architectures built on a transparent conducting oxide and a compact TiO₂ layer. In the upper schematic, light enters a dye-sensitized device containing a mesoporous TiO₂ scaffold coated with orange sensitizer molecules and an overlying mesoporous catalyst. The electrolyte fills the pores, and the visible arrows show the familiar iodide/triiodide redox shuttle. After illumination, electrons are injected into the TiO₂ network and collected at the electrode, while the catalyst and electrolyte regenerate charge through the redox cycle.\n\nThe lower schematic shows a perovskite solar cell using a similar mesoporous TiO₂ framework, but here the perovskite itself forms the light-absorbing layer above and within the scaffold. Instead of relying on a liquid redox electrolyte, the diagram indicates direct generation of electrons and holes in the perovskite: electrons move into the TiO₂ and toward the transparent electrode, while holes travel upward through a hole-transporting material to the back contact.\n\nOverall, the image highlights how perovskite cells evolved from dye-sensitized designs by replacing the sensitizer/electrolyte/catalyst scheme with a solid light absorber and separate hole-transport layer.",
